    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d16">

                <pb facs="0028" n="20" sameAs="frus:frus1938v02/pg20"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">841.4061 Motion Pictures/113: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Kennedy</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-03-11T13:00:00+00:00">March 11, 1938—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 11—8:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>201. Embassy’s 158, February 24, 4 p.m.<anchor n="23" xml:id="d16-f1"/>
                    Amendments to the films bill providing for triple credit and reciprocity credit
                    will be moved by Lord Swinton in House of Lords March 14. The language has been
                    somewhat changed in order to meet some of the objections made by exhibitors
                    groups in House of Commons. Although the new amendments are not so favorable to
                    American interests as those withdrawn in the House of Commons, nevertheless,
                    they do incorporate in the legislation the triple credit provision much desired
                    by American distributors.</p>
                <pb facs="0029" n="21" xml:id="pg21"/>
                <p>Inasmuch as there are likely to be changes in the language of the amendments
                    finally accepted by the Lords, we will cable a full report only after their
                    adoption.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kennedy</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d16-f1" n="23" xml:id="d16-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported that in the House of Commons on February 24 the
                    Government had withdrawn amendments to the films bill providing for triple
                    credit and reciprocity credit in order to prevent an adverse vote (841.4061
                    Motion Pictures/109).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
